# `in [1, 2,]` reports only DS0128; the fix keeps the valid trailing comma.
mode: syntax
---- query
from 'orders.csv' | where amount in [1, 2,]
---- expected
PROGRAM@0..44
  PIPELINE@0..43
    IDENT@0..4 "from"
    WHITESPACE@4..5 " "
    LITERAL@5..17
      STRING@5..17 "'orders.csv'"
    WHITESPACE@17..18 " "
    PIPE@18..19 "|"
    WHITESPACE@19..20 " "
    WHERE_OP@20..43
      IDENT@20..25 "where"
      WHITESPACE@25..26 " "
      IN_EXPR@26..43
        NAME_REF@26..32
          IDENT@26..32 "amount"
        WHITESPACE@32..33 " "
        IDENT@33..35 "in"
        WHITESPACE@35..36 " "
        ARG_LIST@36..43
          L_BRACK@36..37 "["
          LITERAL@37..38
            LONG@37..38 "1"
          COMMA@38..39 ","
          WHITESPACE@39..40 " "
          LITERAL@40..41
            LONG@40..41 "2"
          COMMA@41..42 ","
          R_BRACK@42..43 "]"
  WHITESPACE@43..44 "\n"

error[DS0128]: the list after `in` goes in parentheses
  --> query:1:37
  |
1 | from 'orders.csv' | where amount in [1, 2,]
  |                                     ^ expected `(`
  |
  = help: write the list in parentheses: `in (1, 2,)`

with the machine-applicable fixes applied:
    from 'orders.csv' | where amount in (1, 2,)
which parses without errors
